{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:historian","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:dfdeb0dd97a99f7cd3b96f79fdb71ee5b7306000c3db924d4f64aea1a7a999e7","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.19,"B":0.44,"C":0.17,"D":0.2}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Applied strictly by lexical priority against my OWN CQ3 and CQ2. CONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED: (1) A = P(CQ3 growth >=2.6%). My CQ3 is P10 1.0 / P50 2.05 / P90 2.90, implying right-side sigma ~0.66; P(>=2.6) = P(z >= 0.83) = 0.20. I report 19, within rounding. I did NOT import A's qualitative riders (\"debt stabilizes via growth, broad wage gains\") because the disambiguation lock defines A by growth alone — had I used the riders, A would be lower still, since my CQ1 of 9 denies broad wage gains and my CQ4 decomposition denies easy growth-led debt stabilization. Note the historian tension: >=2.6% was the MAJORITY (~60-65%) of 5-yr OECD+China windows since 1960, so A at 19 is a large downward adjustment, licensed only because the 2A demographic block is labour-input arithmetic rather than forecast. (2) C = P(OECD core >=2.7% AND growth <2.0%). From the CQ2 conjunct-A work, P(core >=2.50%) = 0.35, so P(core >=2.70%) ~0.27. P(growth <2.0%) from my CQ3 = 0.53. Stagflation couples them positively: P(growth<2.0 | core>=2.7) ~0.62. C = 0.27 x 0.62 = 0.167 -> 17. This is coherent with my CQ2 of 20, since both are subsets of the same 27-35% high-core region and CQ2 instead conditions on China deflation (0.60) rather than low growth (0.62). (3) D = P(growth <1.7% AND core <2.0%), disjoint from A and C. P(growth<1.7%) from my CQ3 left side (sigma ~0.82) = P(z < -0.43) = 0.33. P(core <2.0%) ~0.45. The demand-shortfall mechanism couples them positively: P(core<2.0 | growth<1.7) ~0.60. D = 0.33 x 0.60 = 0.20.